===== https://data.source.coop/tristangruppwri/soft-commodity-infrastructure/catalog.json =====
{
  "type": "Catalog",
  "id": "publish",
  "stac_version": "1.1.0",
  "description": "Where Brazilian farms connect to markets. This is the geolocated midstream of\nBrazilian agriculture: the silos, slaughterhouses, dairy plants, and sugar/ethanol\nmills that farms sell into, plus the cooperatives that farms belong to. It exists\nto answer, for any rural property, **who to contact** about deforestation on the\nland — the cooperative that has the farmer as a member, or the buyer that receives\ntheir grain, cattle, or cane.\n\n## How it was made\n\nEvery point comes from a government registry, or is geocoded from one. Nothing is\nguessed from satellite imagery.\n\n- **Grain silos** — CONAB's national warehouse registry (`ArmazensCadastrados`),\n  which every grain warehouse must register with. It ships real GPS coordinates,\n  so these are exact. Includes cooperative, trader/private, and official silos.\n- **Slaughterhouses & dairy plants, federal** — MAPA's SIF (federal meat/dairy\n  inspection) registry. Has the company, tax ID, and address but no coordinates,\n  so we geocoded the addresses ourselves. Beef, poultry, pork, dairy.\n- **Slaughterhouses & dairy plants, state/municipal** — the smaller local plants\n  (SIE/SIM/CONSORCIO) that a frontier producer often delivers to, which the\n  federal list misses. These live in MAPA's SISBI system, which has no bulk\n  download; Trase pulled the SISBI records and geocoded them from the registry\n  addresses, and we use that subset. Registry-derived, not AI-detected.\n- **Sugar/ethanol mills** — ANP's ethanol producer list (company, tax ID, town,\n  capacity). No street address, so each mill sits at its town's center.\n- **Cooperative membership** — IBGE's Censo Agropecuário 2017, which counts how\n  many farms in each município belong to a cooperative. A density signal, not a\n  named cooperative — but it works even in remote frontier towns.\n- **Municipality geometry** — IBGE's official município boundaries.\n\n## Trust labels on every row\n\nBecause not every point is equally precise, each carries two labels. **`basis`**\nis `observed` (someone official recorded it) or `modelled` (we calculated it — only\nthe gravity-catchment rows). **`geom_method`** is `registered` (the source gave us\nthe GPS coordinate — CONAB silos), `geocoded` (we turned an address into a point —\ncan be off), or `municipality_centroid` (all we had was the town). A user can see\nat a glance how good each location is.\n\n## Full methodology & source\n\nPipeline, tests, and a plain-language writeup live at\nhttps://github.com/wri/rural-land (see `soft_commodity_infrastructure/METHODOLOGY.md`).\nBrazil only. Rice, cotton, and coffee are not covered — no authoritative open\ngeolocated source exists for them.\n",
  "links": [
    {
      "rel": "root",
      "href": "./catalog.json",
      "type": "application/json",
      "title": "Soft Commodity Infrastructure (Brazil)"
    },
    {
      "rel": "child",
      "href": "./facilities/collection.json"
    }
  ],
  "title": "Soft Commodity Infrastructure (Brazil)"
}

